1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,000
Cholesterol: It can be good. It can be bad.

2
00:00:04,000 --> 00:00:06,500
Here’s how cholesterol can be good:

3
00:00:06,500 --> 00:00:09,333
Cholesterol is found in all of our cells.

4
00:00:09,333 --> 00:00:13,400
Cells need it to keep their membranes just the right consistency.

5
00:00:13,400 --> 00:00:16,533
Our body also makes things with cholesterol,

6
00:00:16,533 --> 00:00:21,700
like steroid hormones, vitamin D, and bile.

7
00:00:21,700 --> 00:00:24,500
Here’s now cholesterol can be bad:

8
00:00:24,500 --> 00:00:29,933
Cholesterol in the blood can stick to artery walls, forming plaque.

9
00:00:29,933 --> 00:00:32,766
This can block blood flow.

10
00:00:32,766 --> 00:00:39,100
Atherosclerosis is the condition where plaque narrows the space inside the artery.

11
00:00:39,100 --> 00:00:43,300
Multiple factors can cause plaques to rupture, like inflammation.

12
00:00:43,300 --> 00:00:47,400
The body's natural healing response to damaged tissue can cause clots.

13
00:00:47,400 --> 00:00:52,233
If the clots plug up arteries, blood cannot deliver vital oxygen.

14
00:00:52,233 --> 00:00:55,766
If the coronary arteries that feed the heart are blocked,

15
00:00:55,766 --> 00:00:59,133
this could lead to a heart attack.

16
00:00:59,133 --> 00:01:03,800
If the blood vessels of the brain or the carotid arteries of the neck are blocked,

17
00:01:03,800 --> 00:01:06,666
this could lead to a stroke.

18
00:01:06,666 --> 00:01:09,033
If the arteries of the leg are blocked,

19
00:01:09,033 --> 00:01:11,600
this could lead to peripheral artery disease.

20
00:01:11,600 --> 00:01:14,200
This causes painful leg cramps when walking,

21
00:01:14,200 --> 00:01:15,566
numbness and weakness,

22
00:01:15,566 --> 00:01:19,900
or foot sores that do not heal.

23
00:01:19,900 --> 00:01:22,766
So cholesterol can be good and bad.

24
00:01:22,766 --> 00:01:25,833
There are also different types of cholesterol sometimes called 

25
00:01:25,833 --> 00:01:28,666
“good cholesterol” and “bad cholesterol”.

26
00:01:28,666 --> 00:01:34,633
LDL, or low-density lipoprotein, is sometimes called “bad cholesterol”.

27
00:01:34,633 --> 00:01:37,566
It carries cholesterol that can stick to arteries, 

28
00:01:37,566 --> 00:01:39,766
collect in the vessel lining forming plaque,

29
00:01:39,766 --> 00:01:42,233
and sometimes block blood flow

30
00:01:42,233 --> 00:01:48,533
HDL, or high-density lipoprotein, is sometimes called “good cholesterol”.

31
00:01:48,533 --> 00:01:52,733
It takes cholesterol away from the blood and returns it to the liver.

32
00:01:52,733 --> 00:01:57,566
When checked, you want your LDL to be low. "L" for low. 

33
00:01:57,566 --> 00:02:02,100
You want your HDL to be high. "H" for High.

34
00:02:02,100 --> 00:02:06,766
Blood tests can measure the LDL, HDL, and total cholesterol,

35
00:02:06,766 --> 00:02:10,600
Usually, there are no visible symptoms of high cholesterol,

36
00:02:10,600 --> 00:02:13,700
so it is important to be periodically checked.

37
00:02:13,700 --> 00:02:17,800
Ways to decrease your LDL and increase your HDL include:

38
00:02:17,800 --> 00:02:22,066
Eating a heart-healthy diet low in saturated and trans fats.

39
00:02:22,066 --> 00:02:25,233
Regular exercise and being more physically active.

40
00:02:25,233 --> 00:02:27,233
Maintaining healthy weight.

41
00:02:27,233 --> 00:02:28,833
Quitting smoking.

42
00:02:28,833 --> 00:02:32,266
Medications. Medications might be recommended depending on

43
00:02:32,266 --> 00:02:35,300
known risk factors for cardiovascular disease

44
00:02:35,300 --> 00:02:39,200
(such as age and family history among others).

45
00:02:39,200 --> 00:02:43,200
You may already be familiar with these guidelines for heart-healthy living.

46
00:02:43,200 --> 00:02:49,433
They are based on research supported by the National Heart, Lung, and Blood Institute (NHLBI)

47
00:02:49,433 --> 00:02:52,800
at the National Institutes of Health, or NIH.

48
00:02:52,800 --> 00:02:55,333
This video was produced by MedlinePlus,

49
00:02:55,333 --> 00:03:00,000
a trusted source of health information from the U.S. National Library of Medicine.

50
